.TH RETIRE 1 "2026-11-24" "retire 1.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
retire \- set paths aside for later deletion, with a ledger and a safety review
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B retire
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B retire ?
| \fBretire help\fR [\fIcommand\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B retire
renames a path aside instead of deleting it, records what was set aside and when, and
re\-checks it later before anything is removed. It exists so that cleanup is deferred
and reversible rather than immediate and final.
.PP
Nothing in
.B retire
deletes anything except
.BR purge ,
which requires an explicit
.B \-\-yes
and prints what it is about to remove.
.B review
never modifies, moves or deletes a retired path, and never acts on its own verdict. It
does write to its own ledger: the
.I atime
floor described under
.B DETECTION
and any tripwire hits it needs to remember.
.PP
The move itself is the primary tripwire. Once a path is renamed aside, anything that
depended on it fails loudly with ENOENT rather than silently reading stale data. The
checks described under
.B DETECTION
are a secondary signal.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "put " path " " [ \-\-reason " R" "] [" \-\-review\-in " 90d" ]
Rename
.I path
to
.IR path .RETIRED. YYYYMMDD
and record it. The rename is atomic and uses no extra space when the target is on the
same filesystem. The entry is named after the basename of
.IR path ;
if an active entry already has that name, put refuses without moving anything. If the
ledger cannot be written after the rename, put moves the path back.
.TP
.BI "adopt " retired_path " " [ \-\-name " N" "] [" \-\-reason " R" "] [" \-\-review\-in " 90d" ]
Record a path that was already moved aside by hand. The original name is inferred by
stripping a trailing
.B .RETIRED.YYYYMMDD
suffix unless
.B \-\-name
is given.
.TP
.BR list " [" \-a | \-\-all " | " \-\-due "] [" \-\-sort " " \fIkey\fR "] [" \-\-full "] [" \-\-json ]
Show active (retired, not yet purged or restored) entries, sorted by review date
ascending with sizes formatted in human\-readable units. Active entries whose review
date has passed are marked
.BR DUE ;
others are marked
.BR holding .
If any displayed entry has remembered tripwire hits from an earlier
.BR review ,
a
.B HITS
column shows the count. On a terminal, reasons are truncated to fit the width unless
.B \-\-full
is given.
.B \-a\fR/\fB\-\-all
includes purged and restored entries (listed after active ones);
.B \-\-due
restricts output to active entries whose review date has passed;
.B \-\-sort
accepts
.IR review " (default), " retired ", " size " (largest first) or " name ;
.B \-\-full
disables reason truncation and also prints each entry's path and remembered hits;
.B \-\-json
prints the matching entries as a JSON array.
.TP
.BR review " [" \fIname\fR " ...] [" \-\-due "] [" \-\-notify ]
Read\-only safety check over active entries (or only the named entries, or with
.B \-\-due
only those whose review date has passed). When checking multiple entries, the reference
scan walks the search roots once to find candidate files before attributing matches to
individual names, and always skips every active retired path even when only a subset is
reviewed. With
.BR \-\-notify ,
sends a desktop notification when, and only when, at least one entry needs attention.
.TP
.BI "ack " name
Accept the current set of references as known and expected, so that
.B review
flags only references appearing after this point. Use it to silence historical
mentions without losing the ability to detect new ones.
.TP
.BR rebaseline " " \fIname\fR " [" \fIname\fR " ...] | " \-\-all
Reset the tripwire snapshot to each tree's current state and clear remembered hits. Use
after deliberately touching a retired path, or to upgrade entries written by an older
version. With
.BR \-\-all ,
rebaselines every active entry (skipping any whose path is missing on disk with a
warning).
.TP
.BI "restore " name
Move the path back to its original location and mark the entry restored. Refuses if
the original path is occupied.
.TP
.BI "purge " name " " \-\-yes
Delete the retired path for real and mark the entry purged. Without
.BR \-\-yes ,
prints what it would delete and exits without touching anything.
.TP
.B doctor
Report the detected platform, interpreter, config and state locations, which notifier
and grep were found, and whether each configured search root exists. Run this first on
a new machine.
.SH DETECTION
.B review
reports four independent signals.
.TP
.B TOUCHED
A non\-directory entry's
.I atime
(files and symlinks), or any entry's
.IR mtime " or " ctime ,
is later than the recorded baseline:
.I atime
catches file reads,
.I mtime
catches writes, and
.I ctime
catches renames, chmod and chown. For directory trees, the message includes the relative
path of the entry with the newest timestamp.
.IP
Bare directory
.I atime
updates from
.BR readdir (3)
do not trigger
.BR TOUCHED :
directory listings are constantly bumped by tools like
.BR find (1),
.BR du (1),
search tools, desktop indexers, and
.BR review 's
own walk without reading any file contents. Additions, deletions, renames and
permission changes inside directories are still caught by
.IR mtime ", " ctime
and
.BR DRIFT .
Regular files and symlinks are inspected only with
.BR lstat (2),
which never updates their
.IR atime ,
so file reads are compared directly against the original baseline.
.IP
Hits are recorded in the ledger and re\-reported as
.B EARLIER
until
.B rebaseline
clears them.
.IP
.B review
keeps its other reads out of the measurement too. Every tree is scanned before any
reference check runs, and the reference check never enters a retired path. The scan
never follows a symlink, because resolving one updates the link's own
.IR atime .
.TP
.B DRIFT
The entry count or total byte count differs from the baseline, which catches files
added to or deleted from inside the tree.
.TP
.B REFERENCED
A fresh grep of the configured search roots found the retired name in a file that is
not in the acknowledged set. Retired paths are skipped by exact path:
.B retire
lists the directories leading down to each one and hands grep their other children,
filtered as
.B grep \-r
would filter them. A mention inside a retired tree does not count.
.TP
.B UNVERIFIED
A safety check could not complete cleanly: no search root is configured, one or more
configured search roots are missing or unreadable, paths inside the retired tree could
not be scanned, or grep failed, was missing, or timed out. Reported rather than silently
passing as clean.
.PP
An entry is reported as
.B INVESTIGATE
if any of those fire,
.B SAFE TO PURGE
if the review date has passed cleanly, and
.B holding
otherwise.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is optional; every key has a default. The file is JSON at
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/retire/config.json
.RI ( ~/.config/retire/config.json
by default).
.TP
.B search_roots
List of absolute directories to grep for references. Empty by default, which makes
.B review
report
.BR UNVERIFIED .
Set this to the source trees that could plausibly mention a retired path.
.TP
.B includes
Filename globs to search. Defaults to common source and config extensions.
.TP
.B exclude_dirs
Directory names to skip, such as
.I .git
and
.IR node_modules .
.TP
.B atime_slack_hours
Optional grace window before a newer
.I atime
counts as a read, expressed in hours. Defaults to 0 (exact comparison).
.I mtime
and
.I ctime
are exact and never get slack.
.TP
.B default_review_in
Default review horizon for
.B put
and
.BR adopt .
Accepts
.IR Nd ", " Nw ", " Nm " or " Ny .
.PP
Example:
.PP
.EX
{
  "search_roots": ["/home/me/src", "/srv/analysis"],
  "atime_slack_hours": 0,
  "default_review_in": "6m"
}
.EE
.SH PLATFORM
The core \(em ledger, snapshot comparison and every command \(em is portable POSIX
Python needing only Python 3.7 or newer and no third\-party packages.
.TP
.B Linux
Fully supported. Notifications use
.BR notify-send (1);
because cron supplies no session environment,
.B retire
fills in
.B DISPLAY
and
.B DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
itself.
.TP
.B macOS
Fully supported. Notifications use
.B terminal-notifier
when installed, otherwise
.BR osascript .
Schedule with launchd rather than cron; see
.I contrib/
in the source distribution. Note that APFS records access times differently from a
Linux
.B relatime
mount, so the
.I atime
signal is less predictable there;
.IR mtime ", " ctime
and the count checks are unaffected.
.TP
.B Other
On any platform with no notifier,
.B review
still runs and writes its verdict to the log; only the desktop popup is lost. Windows
is not supported.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RETIRE_STATE
Directory holding the ledger and logs. Defaults to
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/retire .
Set it to exercise
.B retire
against a scratch ledger without disturbing the real one.
.TP
.B RETIRE_CONFIG
Path to the config file.
.TP
.B RETIRE_SEARCH_ROOTS
Search roots as a
.BR os.pathsep -separated
list, overriding the config file.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.TP
.B No attribution
The timestamp checks tell you that something touched the tree, never who or which
process. For attribution, place an audit watch on the path instead:
.IP
.EX
sudo auditctl \-w /path/to/thing.RETIRED.20260101 \-p rwa \-k retire
sudo ausearch \-k retire
.EE
.TP
.B atime granularity
On a filesystem mounted
.BR relatime ,
a read updates
.I atime
at most once per day, hence the default slack. A filesystem mounted
.B noatime
will not record reads at all, leaving
.IR mtime ", " ctime
and the count checks as the only signals.
.TP
.B False positives
Backup, indexing, search and antivirus tools that open and read file contents (even a
few header bytes to sniff binary vs. text) still bump file
.IR atime .
Bare directory listings
.RB ( readdir )
are ignored. The relative path printed in each
.B TOUCHED
line helps distinguish data reads from scanner noise.
.TP
.B Short names are noisy
.B REFERENCED
matches the retired name as a whole word, literally. A short or common name will
still match unrelated prose. Use
.B ack
to accept the current set once you have read it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/retire/ledger.jsonl
One JSON object per entry: paths, dates, size, reason, baseline snapshot,
acknowledged references, status.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/retire/review.log
Every review verdict, appended.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/retire/config.json
Optional configuration.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set a directory aside for six months:
.PP
.EX
retire put /srv/old_cache \-\-reason "superseded by parquet" \-\-review\-in 6m
.EE
.PP
Check everything by hand, then silence historical mentions:
.PP
.EX
retire review
retire ack old_cache
.EE
.PP
Delete once the review comes back clean:
.PP
.EX
retire purge old_cache \-\-yes
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.BR review ,
no entry needs attention.
.TP
.B 1
An error, or for
.B review
at least one entry is
.B INVESTIGATE
or
.BR "SAFE TO PURGE" .
.TP
.B 2
Unknown command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR auditctl (8),
.BR ausearch (8),
.BR notify-send (1),
.BR crontab (5),
.BR launchd.plist (5),
.BR trash-put (1)
